<!-- ═══════════════ COVER ═══════════════ -->
<div class="cover">
  <div class="cover-tag">Trend Analysis Report · 2015–2035</div>
  <h1>AI Impact on the Global Job Market:<br>Risk, Salary &amp; Skills Analysis</h1>
  <p class="cover-sub">
    A comprehensive examination of how AI automation risk, salary dynamics, skill demand,
    and job survival prospects are evolving across roles, countries, and experience levels
    over a 21-year horizon.
  </p>
  <div class="cover-meta">
    <span><span class="dot"></span> 12,343 records analyzed</span>
    <span><span class="dot"></span> 10 job titles · 6 countries</span>
    <span><span class="dot"></span> April 2026</span>
    <span><span class="dot"></span> Executive Edition</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════ NAV ═══════════════ -->
<nav class="toc-bar" aria-label="Section navigation">
  <div class="toc-inner">
    <a href="#executive-overview">Executive Overview</a>
    <a href="#automation-risk">Automation Risk Landscape</a>
    <a href="#salary-trends">Salary Trends &amp; Segmentation</a>
    <a href="#skills-demand">Skills Demand &amp; Future-Proofing</a>
    <a href="#job-survival">Job Survival &amp; Market Outlook</a>
    <a href="#recommendations">Strategic Recommendations</a>
  </div>
</nav>

<div class="page-wrapper">

<!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW ═══════════════ -->
<section id="executive-overview" class="section">
  <div class="section-label">01 — Executive Overview</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">The AI Workforce Inflection Point</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    This report synthesizes 12,343 records spanning 2015–2035 across 10 job categories, 6 countries, and
    9 skill domains. The data reveals a workforce in structural transition — where automation risk is rising
    uniformly, but compensation, survival prospects, and skill demand are diverging sharply by role.
    Five findings demand immediate executive attention.
  </p>

  <!-- Top-line KPIs -->
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card accent-rose">
      <div class="kpi-label">Global Avg. AI Risk Score</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">0.396</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Up from 0.295 in 2015 → 0.508 by 2035</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card accent-indigo">
      <div class="kpi-label">Overall Median Salary</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">$31,573</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">+51% projected growth by 2035 (USD)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card accent-emerald">
      <div class="kpi-label">Job Survival Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">89.0%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Declining: 91.1% (2015) → 83.7% (2035)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card accent-sky">
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg. Skill Demand Score</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">79.5<span style="font-size:16px;color:#64748b">/100</span></div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Python fastest-growing (+3.0 pts vs 2015)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card accent-amber">
      <div class="kpi-label">High-Risk Role Workers</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">18.6%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Business Analysts &amp; Data Analysts most exposed</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Critical Findings -->
  <h3 class="subsection">Five Critical Findings for Leadership</h3>
  <div class="callout-grid">
    <div class="callout risk">
      <div class="callout-icon">⚠️</div>
      <div class="callout-title">Finding 1 — Automation Risk is Rising Universally</div>
      <p>Mean AI risk score climbed from <strong>0.295 in 2015</strong> to a projected <strong>0.508 by 2035</strong> — a 72% increase. No job category is immune; all roles show a uniform +0.20 risk increase over the 20-year window.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="callout risk">
      <div class="callout-icon">📉</div>
      <div class="callout-title">Finding 2 — Business &amp; Data Analysts Face Existential Risk</div>
      <p>Business Analysts carry the highest average risk score (<strong>0.75</strong>) with <strong>57% already classified as "at risk"</strong> (survival class 0). Data Analysts follow at 0.65 risk with 43.5% at risk. These roles urgently need reskilling pipelines.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="callout safe">
      <div class="callout-icon">🛡️</div>
      <div class="callout-title">Finding 3 — High Risk ≠ High Pay; Low Risk = Premium Salaries</div>
      <p>High-risk roles earn a median of <strong>$21,683</strong> — less than half the <strong>$34,105–$34,710</strong> earned by low- and medium-risk roles. AI-resilient careers command a <strong>59% salary premium</strong> over high-risk counterparts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="callout safe">
      <div class="callout-icon">🚀</div>
      <div class="callout-title">Finding 4 — AI Researchers &amp; ML Engineers are Structurally Immune</div>
      <p>AI Researchers show <strong>88.9% strong survival</strong> (class 2) with 0% at-risk classification. ML Engineers follow at 71.2% strong survival. Deep Learning and Python skills are the strongest future-proofing assets in the dataset.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="callout warn">
      <div class="callout-icon">📊</div>
      <div class="callout-title">Finding 5 — Job Survival Rate is in Slow Decline</div>
      <p>The overall workforce survival rate fell from <strong>91.1% in 2015</strong> to <strong>83.7% in 2035</strong> — a 7.4-point erosion. The sharpest drop occurs post-2024, coinciding with rapid AI capability gains. Proactive intervention can reverse this trajectory.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="callout info">
